Subject: [amelia] request for advice on imputing data from an experiment
In-Reply-To: <AANLkTik4NX4MRy5Rm1jQ-v4Fzsi5KQo9DVGS55qu6yzP@mail.gmail.com>
References: <AANLkTi=XxYTkMTqQekqH4LDXgezeKFS6KqNVZDZFuTvq@mail.gmail.com> <AANLkTinT78uxSrBb44vqkM5HNHoNFzCX1PMbzBxuFWAd@mail.gmail.com> <AANLkTik4NX4MRy5Rm1jQ-v4Fzsi5KQo9DVGS55qu6yzP@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <AANLkTik5VX1hHYj2VddzFGEqyc_vQABVJNuUGHBuKt9=@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Donald,

You should impute all of the variables and their interactions being
careful not to include linear combinations of the variables (that is,
indicators for every category of a nominal variable, always omit one).
Then, after the fact, you should manually edit the imputed
interactions to be the interaction of the individual imputations.
Thus, if varX_k is imputation k for varX, and (1*varX)_k is the kth
imputation of the interaction, you should replace (1*varX)_k with
1*(varX_k). Hope that helps.

Cheers,
matt.

